 "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 26.3, "width": 67.6, "height": 59.5, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 26.3, "width": 67.6, "height": 59.5, "rotation": 0, "text": ["The reasons for using energy more efficiently are clear: to relieve pressure on scarce energy resources, to reduce energy costs by avoiding wastefulness, and perhaps most pressing, to reduce energy related carbon dioxide $( \\mathsf { C O } _ { 2 } )$ emissions which contribute to climate change. The well-known Kaya identity [1] expresses the generation of energy-based $C 0 _ { 2 }$ emissions as the product of four drivers: population, per capita wealth, energy intensity (energy per unit wealth) and carbon intensity( $\\mathbf { C O } _ { 2 }$ per unit energy). The first two drivers are socio-economic and are difficult to limit in practice. The third and fourth drivers are technical options which require energy to be used more efficiently (which lowers energy intensity) and the decarbonisation of energy supplies (which reduces carbon intensity). "]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}], "created_username": "14. NAIST nlp_annot_14@naist.ac.jp, 20", "created_ago": "0\u00a0minutes", "completed_by": {"id": 20, "first_name": "14. NAIST", "last_name": "", "email": "nlp_annot_14@naist.ac.jp"}, "task": {"id": 140775, "data": {"ocr": "/lectrans/data/local-files/?d=/large/datas/frederikus-h/lectrans-testset/25.10.annot_tools/label-studio/ocr_check_storage_source/lec_slides/urban_energy_systems_and_policy/u1_Wq00b4J8/10.u1_Wq00b4J8-Scene-296-03.jpg", "text": "/lectrans/data/local-files/?d=/large/dat